      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of rows in the can display, we recognize that the number of cans in each row forms an arithmetic sequence. The sequence starts with the first row having 1 can and each subsequent row having twice as many cans as the previous row. The general formula for the \\( n \\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\na_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n\\]\n\nHere, \\( a_1 \\) is the first term (1 can), and \\( d \\) is the common difference (2 cans per row). The total number of cans in the display is 100, so we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[\na_n = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2\n\\]\n\nWe also know that the sum of the first \\( n \\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\n\\[\nS_n = \\frac{n}{2} (2a_1 + (n-1)d)\n\\]\n\nFor this problem, we know \\( S_n = 100 \\), \\( a_1 = 1 \\), and \\( d = 2 \\):\n\n\\[\n100 = \\frac{n}{2} (2 \\cdot 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 2)\n\\]\n\nSimplify inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\n100 = \\frac{n}{2} (2 + 2n - 2)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n100 = \\frac{n}{2} \\cdot 2n\n\\]\n\n\\[\n100 = n^2\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( n \\):\n\n\\[\nn = \\sqrt{100} = 10\n\\]\n\nThus, the display contains \\(\\boxed{10}\\) rows.",
